From: Escapement
Subject: [PATCH] watch: quad PSRAM, and keep space_for_vectors on the kernel's page

The T-Watch Ultra has 8 MiB of *quad* PSRAM, not octal. Upstream targets
N16R8 in its octal sense, so the stock firmware aborts in the app before
Linux is ever reached:

    E (173) octal_psram: PSRAM ID read error: 0x00000000, PSRAM chip not
            found or not supported, or wrong PSRAM line mode
    E (173) cpu_start: Failed to init external RAM!

The bootloader is not involved: the release bootloader is byte-identical
whatever mode is configured, and the failure tag is app-stage. Only the app
needs rebuilding and only 0x10000 needs reflashing.

Second hunk: quad mode changes how much internal RAM the firmware uses, and
that moves `space_for_vectors` -- which the kernel is linked against as
CONFIG_VECTORS_ADDR=0x40380000. The symbol is a 4 KiB buffer declared
4 KiB-aligned, so with the allocation landing one page lower it rounded down
to 0x4037f000 and the kernel would jump to the wrong page. Asking for 8 KiB
alignment rounds it back up to 0x40380000, which avoids rebuilding the
kernel. Check it holds after any change to internal RAM use:

    xtensa-esp32s3-elf-nm build/network_adapter.elf | grep space_for_vectors
    # must print 40380000

--- a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3.16m8r
+++ b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3.16m8r
@@ -660,8 +660,8 @@
 
 CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
 
-# CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_QUAD is not set
-CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT=y
+CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_QUAD=y
+# CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT is not set
 CONFIG_SPIRAM_TYPE_AUTO=y
 # CONFIG_SPIRAM_TYPE_ESPPSRAM64 is not set
 CONFIG_SPIRAM_ALLOW_STACK_EXTERNAL_MEMORY=y
--- a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
+++ b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
@@ -54,7 +54,7 @@
 }
 #endif
 
-static char IRAM_ATTR space_for_vectors[4096] __attribute__((aligned(4096)));
+static char IRAM_ATTR space_for_vectors[4096] __attribute__((aligned(8192)));
 
 #define N_TAGS (3 + CMDLINE_MAX / sizeof(struct bp_tag))
 
